{% comment %}
  Self-hosts Overused Grotesk (OFL-1.1) as the body + heading family for the
  whole theme. Render after the layout's {% style %} block so these :root
  overrides win. Source: github.com/RandomMaerks/Overused-Grotesk
{% endcomment %}

{% comment %}theme-check-disable AssetPreload{% endcomment %}
<link rel="preload" as="font" href="{{ 'overused-grotesk-variable.woff2' | asset_url }}" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>
{% comment %}theme-check-enable AssetPreload{% endcomment %}

{% style %}
  @font-face {
    font-family: 'Overused Grotesk';
    src: url({{ 'overused-grotesk-variable.woff2' | asset_url }}) format('woff2-variations'),
         url({{ 'overused-grotesk-variable.woff2' | asset_url }}) format('woff2');
    font-weight: 300 900;
    font-style: normal;
    font-display: swap;
  }

  {% comment %}
    Italics come from the variable font's `slnt` axis (-10deg to 0), which
    covers the full 300-900 weight range -- the static italic file is Regular
    only, so "Light Italic" could not render from it. It stays as the fallback
    src for browsers without variable-font support. CSS oblique angles run
    opposite in sign to slnt; the browser handles that mapping.
  {% endcomment %}
  @font-face {
    font-family: 'Overused Grotesk';
    src: url({{ 'overused-grotesk-variable.woff2' | asset_url }}) format('woff2-variations'),
         url({{ 'overused-grotesk-italic.woff2' | asset_url }}) format('woff2');
    font-weight: 300 900;
    font-style: oblique 0deg 10deg;
    font-display: swap;
  }

  :root {
    --font-body-family: 'Overused Grotesk', -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
    --font-body-style: normal;
    --font-body-weight: 400;
    --font-body-weight-bold: 600;

    --font-heading-family: 'Overused Grotesk', -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
    --font-heading-style: normal;
    --font-heading-weight: 500;

    /* Every named instance on the variable font's wght axis (300-900).
       Read from the file's fvar table, so these match the designer's own
       naming -- note Book is 350, which is not a standard CSS keyword and
       has to be written numerically. */
    --font-weight-light: 300;
    --font-weight-book: 350;
    --font-weight-regular: 400;
    --font-weight-medium: 500;
    --font-weight-semibold: 600;
    --font-weight-bold: 700;
    --font-weight-extrabold: 800;
    --font-weight-black: 900;
  }
{% endstyle %}
